>
	<p>
		I’m Dan Scotti, and I’ve spent the last 30 years working in IT across infrastructure engineering, systems administration, cloud architecture, datacenter operations, virtualization, and network security. Over the years, I’ve worked at places like MathWorks, Hanover Insurance, and Interactive Data, where keeping systems running and protecting sensitive financial data wasn’t theoretical—it was part of the daily grind. I have also spent time in manufacturing and K-12 education.  Looking back I’ve had a pretty broad exposure across many types of industries. I have often described my career as a hobby that ran amok. 
	</p>
	
	<p>	
		I’ve always liked understanding how things actually work under the hood, from out-of-band management, to locking down networks and digging into new tools. I finished both the Google Cybersecurity Professional Certificate and the Google AI Professional Certificate, and I’m still looking into things like Microsoft’s SC-500 path, and ethical hacking to keep my natural curiosity satisfied.
	</p>

	<p>
		Outside of work, I tinker plenty. At home, I run a setup on Ubuntu Server using Docker Compose alongside OPNsense and TrueNAS SCALE with ZFS for Kubernetes apps, plus things like Pi-hole, Unbound, Caddy, WireGuard, Vaultwarden, Netdata, and ntfy alerts hooked up to Home Assistant. I am a fan of open scource products. Partly because I’m a cheapskate, but mostly because I love the sense of community open source provides. 
	</p>
	
	<p>	
		When I step away from screens, I’m usually in the shop working on wooden projects for my small woodworking business <a href="https://www.scottidogwoodworking.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
		ScottiDog Woodworking
	</a>
		, out on the water sailing when the weather’s right, or listening to/watching my favorite teams on TV and radio. 
	</p>
	
	<p>	
		I started this site to keep notes, thoughts and experiences on what I’m building, troubleshooting, and learning along the way. If sharing any of my experience helps you fix a problem or gives you an idea, even better. I will do my best to keep this blog true to my experiences, and give my honest thoughts and feedback on the whole thing. I also will from time to time weigh in on current events in the technology world like outages, hacks, new tech, and anything else that catches my fancy. That is a benefit of having your own blog. You can write about whatever you want.
	</p>
</Layout>
